'''Kenichi Mori''' ({{lang|ja|森賢一}} ''Mori Kenichi''; born 15 March 1968) is a Sanese politician serving as [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|Minister of Foreign Affairs]] since 20 May 2026, in the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] of Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]]. A member of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) representing the [[Okami Metropolis]] proportional representation bloc in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]], he has held the foreign portfolio through the [[Wolffrean War]] crisis of 2026, in which Okaiken has maintained a policy of armed neutrality.

Mori is the architect of what the diplomatic press has labelled the "receipt doctrine" — his formulation that "an abstention is a receipt" — and of the government's insistence that Okaiken's wartime conduct is governed by the [[Ninth Amendment]] to the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] rather than by the command structures of the [[Adonian Community]]. Under his direction Okaiken has paid for the refugee sealift while voting against every resolution that would commit Sanese forces to the war, drafted [[Adonian Community Resolution 4942D|Resolution 4942D]] to codify the safety corridors, and issued the 25 October 2026 government response rejecting demands that the [[Escort Flotilla]] be placed under Community or coalition command. He survived a motion of no confidence moved against him on 24 October 2026, defeated 248–224.

== Early life and education ==
Mori was born on 15 March 1968 in [[Okami]], the son of a stevedore working the Kumagawa Delta piers and a primary school teacher. He was educated at Okami municipal schools and read law at the [[University of Okami]], graduating in 1991. His graduation thesis examined the Ninth Amendment — then seven years old — and argued that the clause prohibiting overseas combat deployment "even under Adonian Community authorization" was the constitution's most honest sentence, because it named the institution most likely to ask for the deployment. Examiners' minutes record the paper as "adversarial but careful", an assessment colleagues have applied to its author ever since.

== Early career ==
After graduation Mori spent seven years as a legal officer with the Okami Dockworkers' Federation, handling wage arbitration and safety cases arising from the delta ports. He entered the [[Okami Metropolis]] Assembly in 1998 and chaired its harbour committee from 2002, where he compiled a record on port safety and seafarers' welfare that he has cited in every maritime file he has touched since.

== House of Commoners ==
Mori was elected to the House of Commoners in 2005 through the Okami Metropolis PR bloc and has been returned at every general election since, always through the same bloc. He sat on the Foreign Affairs Committee from his first term and served as the OSP's spokesperson for foreign affairs from 2013 until his appointment to the Cabinet, a long apprenticeship spent almost entirely in opposition.

His parliamentary record is marked by two consistent threads. The first is support for the humanitarian machinery of the [[Adonian Community]] — dues, relief contributions, refugee resettlement — paired with opposition to every proposal to deploy Sanese personnel under a Community command. The second is constitutional: in 2014 Mori was among the OSP members who voted against the Thirteenth Amendment's revision of Article 9, warning in committee that the new language — forces maintained "to protect Okaiken's most valuable assets" — was "a licence written in the language of insurance". Colleagues on both sides read his conduct as Foreign Minister as an attempt to keep the 2014 text exactly as narrow as he warned it should be kept.

== Foreign Ministership ==
=== Appointment ===
Mori was appointed Minister of Foreign Affairs on 20 May 2026, taking the OSP's senior portfolio in the coalition cabinet formed by [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] after the March 2026 snap election. He inherited a war eleven weeks old, a refugee catastrophe of more than seventeen million displaced, and the coldest bilateral relationship in the archipelago's diplomatic record.

=== The Wolffrean War ===
Mori's first months in office were dominated by the [[Wolffrean War]]. In June 2026, after the [[Mzinti attack]] — the delivery of VX nerve agent against [[Front for the Liberation of Ukumenguza|FLU]] positions — he told the House that "a state that gases the people it refuses to count as citizens has forfeited the right to be called a state; it has not forfeited the obligation to answer for it." In July he announced that the humanitarian admission quota for Wolffrean refugees would rise from 300 to 3,000 per year and that compatibility screening would end.

The September votes were cast under his direction: in favour of the sanctions resolutions, an abstention that defeated the Sindewand sanctions draft on 28 September, a vote against the proposed unified Community ground force, and an abstention on the authorization of the [[JWG-PNK]]. He defended the pattern at a press conference with the formulation that has followed him since: "an abstention is a receipt. It is a record that the Assembly asked, and of what was asked, and of what we declined."

The [[Aozora Maru]] attack of 16 October and the deconfliction leak of 20 October drew him into the chamber. In the Diet debate of 23–24 October he rebutted the claim by Staatsrat Chairman Kruse-Essert that Okaiken had drawn targeting maps for coalition aviation — "That is an outright falsehood. Our consular mission in Yaweka passed transit coordinates for one purpose: to ensure that civilian hulls carrying desperate families were not blown out of the water by either side" — and announced the submission of [[Adonian Community Resolution 4942D|Resolution 4942D]], declaring that "when covert coordination is warped into wartime propaganda, the only remedy is radical transparency." The opposition moved a motion of no confidence against him on 24 October, alleging "criminal diplomatic negligence" over the leaked coordinates; it was defeated 248–224.

At the emergency night sitting of 24 October he opened for the government, describing the Sindewand sanctions draft as "a prosecution brief in search of a crime" and defending the escort rules of engagement as the most restrained ever enacted by a member state: "Our destroyers may intercept shells already in the air and silence the battery that fired them. They may do nothing else. The Committee on Resolutions is invited to name the article of the Charter that a ferry violates."

On 25 October, with demands that the Escort Flotilla be brought under Community or coalition command pressed from three directions, the Ministry issued the government's formal response in Mori's name — the fullest public formulation of the [[Fortress State]] doctrine, invoking the 925 Sanese personnel who had served under a Community command at [[Adonian Community Mission for Lacashe|Lacashe]] and declaring Okaiken "bound by the treaties it has signed, and by nothing else". The statement and its context are set out in [[Sanese involvement in the Wolffrean War]].

== Political positions ==
Mori is a multilateralist in finance and a unilateralist in force. He supports the Adonian Community's humanitarian and legal machinery without reservation and has defended the archipelago's dues record against both flanks of the Diet. He opposes, on constitutional grounds, every structure that would place Sanese forces under a foreign command, and has described the [[Adonian Community Military]]'s investigation of its own operations as "a court that reports to the accused". His stated remedy for the collapse of backroom coordination is "radical transparency" — codifying safety corridors into Assembly law rather than exchanging them through confidential cables.

The press has called his vote pattern the "receipt doctrine", after his own gloss on the abstentions. Mori accepts the label with the qualification he applies to most labels: "It is accurate, and it is not the whole of it."

== Personal life ==
Mori is married to Chiho Mori, a hospital administrator, and has two children. The family lives in Okami. He is an accomplished amateur shogi player and is known among the press corps for delivering one-line answers without looking up from his notes.

